WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.139
It's been a year

00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:06.740
since Russia first occupied Crimea and
nearly a year since

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.360
a full-blown separatist conflict broke
out in eastern Ukraine

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.789
We in the West

00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:17.640
tend to look at this conflict solely
through the lens Russian aggression but

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.290
there's so much more to understand.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:23.040
you need to understand Ukraine's history
and in particular

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.700
how that history has left the country
with deep politica,l

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.859
linguistic, economic, just fundamental
national divisions.

00:00:32.859 --> 00:00:37.199
So we can see this first of all by just
looking at a pretty basic political map

00:00:37.199 --> 00:00:37.940
of Ukraine.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.899
If you look at the result in the
election that brought Yanukovych to

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:43.679
power, you can see that

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:48.050
the eastern regions of the country were
far more likely to support Yanukovych,

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:52.949
and the western regions were far more
likely to support Tymochenko.

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:56.999
And the interesting thing about that is
the that political divide that you see

00:00:56.999 --> 00:01:01.710
comes up in a ton of different aspects of
Ukrainian's history and geography.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:05.089
So one area is the linguistic divide.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:07.969
The divides the different regions of the
country that are predominately

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:09.200
Russian-speaking

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.229
as opposed to predominantly Ukranian
speaking really closely mirrored that

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:16.729
political divide.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:22.020
Russia went through a series of policies to do what they called "Russification"

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.899
in the 1700s under Catherine the Great, so
this goes back hundreds and hundreds of years

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.869
those policies involved everything from

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:31.979
settling ethnic Russians in those
regions to

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:35.490
forbidding people from speaking the
Ukrainian language. 

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.090
It continued well into the 20th century.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.340
Stalin essentially orchestrated this massive famine that

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.720
killed millions of people in Ukraine.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.119
After that, they increase the resettlement of Russians into Ukraine.

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:51.979
You were left with a Ukraine that looked
different. Parts of the country were much

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:54.020
more likely to speak Russian,

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:58.619
be culturally Russian, be ethnically
Russian, and it's had a lasting impact

00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:02.069
on the country as a whole. Fast-forward to modern Ukraine.

00:02:02.069 --> 00:02:05.110
When Yanukovych was elected, 

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:10.069
he was negotiating with the EU about a deal that would have brought closer EU ties.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:14.250
So then when he abandoned that and took a bailout from Russia instead,

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.660
that was perceived as him really kinda
turning away from the west

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.310
and turning towards Russia. And that was
very controversial.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.840
It prompted protests that were largely
clustered in the western part of the

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.319
country and the capital city Kiev.

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:30.970
And eventually it forced Yanukovych
out of office.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.920
Point is that this is not just Russia invading Ukraine

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.880
The point is that there are Ukrainian
separatists

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:41.920
who are actively trying to

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.800
achieved independence from Kiev.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.400
Different parts of ukraine have a very
different idea what kind of country

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.799
Ukraine should be. And that doesn't make
it OK--that doesn't make it acceptable

00:02:50.799 --> 00:02:54.450
as the matter of international law. It
certainly doesn't justify 

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.120
what Russia has been doing,

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.870
but it also shouldn't be something that you
ignore.

